<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Apalachee chiefdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1704 CE (approx.) · capital Anhaica</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A maize-farming chiefdom of the Florida Panhandle that met de Soto, became the heart of Spain's mission system and was destroyed by English-led raids in 1704.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Apalachee emerged from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-walton-culture">Fort Walton culture</a>, the Mississippian tradition of the region between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aucilla-river">Aucilla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ochlockonee-river">Ochlockonee</a> rivers around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tallahassee">Tallahassee</a>, from around 1100. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-jackson-mounds">Lake Jackson</a> mounds were an early centre. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panfilo-de-narvaez">Pánfilo de Narváez</a> arrived in 1528 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a> wintered at their capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anhaica">Anhaica</a>, in 1539 to 1540, the Apalachee were populous and warlike.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chiefs ruled from towns with mounds and plazas, and the fertile red clay hills produced large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> surpluses. The Apalachee were famous for a ball game with ritual meaning. Franciscan missions began in 1633, and many Apalachee became Catholic, sending maize and labour to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-augustine-florida">St Augustine</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mission-san-luis-de-talimali">Mission San Luis de Talimali</a>, founded in 1656, became the capital of western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida</a>, housing Apalachee leaders alongside Spanish soldiers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1704 Carolina's former governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-moore-carolina-governor">James Moore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Muscogee</a> allies destroyed most missions, killing or enslaving thousands. Survivors scattered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a>, to Mobile in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-louisiana">French Louisiana</a>, or joined the Muscogee. Descendants survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talimali-band-of-apalachee">Talimali Band of Apalachee</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/apalachee-chiefdom.jpg" alt="Apalachee chiefdom" width="320" height="256"><figcaption>Image: Henry Popple · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ApalacheeRegion1733.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1704 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Anhaica</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mississippian religion, later Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Apalachee</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Apalachee, Apalachee Province</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1402&amp;focus=apalachee-chiefdom" aria-label="Apalachee chiefdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2495 1067 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Apalachee chiefdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2815 1323L2886 1342M2815 1323L2935 1168"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2886 1342h0M2935 1168h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2815 1323h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2815 1323h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Anhaica. <a href="/atlas?year=1402&amp;focus=apalachee-chiefdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

